      </div>

      <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Defines a Tang Suit</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">The mandarin (stand-up) collar, front-opening with knotted frog buttons (pan kou), and a straight, boxy cut that sits comfortably on most body types. Traditional versions use silk brocade with auspicious patterns — dragons, clouds, longevity characters. Modern versions come in linen, cotton, and wool blends for everyday wear. Colors carry meaning: red for celebrations and weddings, black with gold for formal occasions, navy for business settings. A well-made Tang suit jacket costs 200-500 RMB at Beijing tailor shops and takes 2-4 days to complete.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where to Get One in Beijing</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">The best Tang suit tailors cluster in Beijing''s remaining hutong neighborhoods. Nanluoguxiang has tourist-friendly shops with English-speaking staff and quicker turnaround. For a more authentic experience, head to the tailor shops near Dashilar and Qianmen — family-run operations that have been cutting silk for generations. Prices are 20-30% lower in these older neighborhoods, and the craftsmanship is often superior. Pro tip: bring a photo of the style you want. Most tailors can replicate any Tang suit design from a reference image.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Wear It</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">Chinese New Year is the peak season — red Tang suits appear everywhere from family dinners to temple fairs. Weddings call for elaborate silk brocade with gold embroidery. For travelers, a made-to-measure Tang suit makes an extraordinary souvenir: it''s wearable, packable, and carries a story. Many men wear their Tang suit jacket as a distinctive dinner jacket back home. It pairs well with dark jeans for casual settings or dress trousers for semi-formal events.</p>
